  "worked_solution": "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm \\pi ,\u2026\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that we want to find the area between the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y$ and the $y$-axis from $y=0$ to $y=\\pi .$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0) \\\\  & =-(-1)+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
